Phase 2 — Branch: Control flow to the sink is a single path: line 13 → line 14 → line 17. Reverse protection scan finds `if (index < 0 || index >= size) { return -1; }` at line 14 on that only path. Its admitted set is exactly `[0, size-1]` when `size >= 1`, and empty when `size <= 0` (any non-negative index satisfies `index >= size`; negatives satisfy `index < 0`). Guard-identity audit: the guard constrains the same `int` value `index` consumed at line 17, with no assignment or transformation between check and use — no drift class applies. Degenerate inputs: `-1`/`INT_MIN` are excluded by `index < 0`; `INT_MAX` and any `index >= size` are excluded by `index >= size`; `size = 0` or negative makes every index return `-1`, so the sink is unreachable with `size <= 0`.

Phase 3 — Loop: The analyzed helper contains no loop; the caller's fill loop (`for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)`, line 31) is harness context outside the helper fragment and bounds `i` identically to the allocation size. No iteration-bound or last-index issue exists within the fragment.

Phase 4 — Per-sink resolution: Sink `prices[index];` at line 17 has failure set `index ∉ [0, size-1]`. Guard at line 14 admits exactly `[0, size-1]`, so the intersection with the failure set is empty. BREAK test: (1) identity — same value/type, no intervening modification; (2) boundary — largest admitted index is `size-1`, capacity is `size`, so no collision; (3) degenerate — `-1`, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, `size<=0` all excluded as shown; (4) reach — the guard executes on the only path to the sink, before it, unconditionally. Resolution: PRESENT. No other dangerous operation exists in the fragment, so the conjunction is safe. Fix-Presence conclusion: the single inventoried sink is PRESENT with line-14 guard excluding `index < 0` and `index >= size`; this is decisive for the NOT-VULNERABLE verdict.

