Off-by-one conventions: `i < N` admits 0..N-1; `i <= N` admits N and is unsafe for any sink indexing an N-element object with i; `i > 0` admits i == 1; `count - 1` and `i - 1` fire on empty/zero inputs;a string's NUL slot needs capacity N+1 when N is the copy payload length. A guard must protectthe EXACT later use, including the use one step after the check.



CAPACITY-EVIDENCE RULE (mandatory for every OOB claim,:the capacity of the object must be established by VERBATIM QUOTE of its declaration or allocation — a static array size, `malloc(N)`, a struct field sized in the shown fragment, or an exact computed size whose derivation line is quoted. "Capacity implied by an unrelated guard," "the array is well-known to have N entries," or "no capacity shown but the index is obviously large" are NON-EVIDENCE, whichever direction the verdict uses them. Without a quoted capacity line, an OOB candidate is demoted to `notes.limitations` regardless of how large the index appears; conversely, a guard that admits a value up to the QUOTED capacity exactly is safe unless another quoted line contradicts it. When a loop indexes MULTIPLE objects with the same variable, derive EACH object's capacity from its OWN declaration — two arrays can have different sizes under one loop bound。




